        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a346"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts319" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts322">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah. Hey, we're a two-way street. [I know]</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a423"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts326" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts327">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Oh.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a347"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts333" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts335">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>There's so much. Um, definitely your childhood.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a348"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts337" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts339">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Losing your mom. That's something that, like, I feel. You know how they say, like, generational trauma.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a349"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts343" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts346">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Like, I feel the pain of the loss of your mom. Losing her twice, I mean, to her first brain aneurysm and then living with a different mom and then losing her in your 20s permanently.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a350"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts349" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts352">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Hum. Just like you experience so much trauma at such a young age. And as the daughter of a mother, you don't realize that until you're an adult.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a351"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts355" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts357">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>As I got older, I was able to see you, not as my mom, but just as a human living in this world. And it allowed me to have a lot of empathy for you and understanding.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a352"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts359" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts361">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>And I think it changed my mindset. You know, that's when you go from like a kid to an adult and you can have this more friendship relationship. And I always think about these are the years you didn't get with your mom. </ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a353"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts366" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts369">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah. And I never took that for granted. I feel like I started to appreciate you more because I knew that we were having an experience that you never had. You know, we get to be adults together, like full-formed past 25 adults, you know?</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a354"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts370" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts373">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>And hum.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a355"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts375" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts378">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>I think it just makes me look at you in such a different way. I'm able to see you as my mom, this comfort, this soft landing for me always. But then I also see you as this flawed human, which I think is really important. Like, there's this thing that's kind of going around social media you see sometimes where people say, like, this is a moment where I realize my mom or my parents, this is their first time doing life. And you kind of had this epiphany of like,</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a356"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts381" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts384">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>This is your first take of this, just like it's my first take. Here there is a 30-year difference between us, but like we're both doing it for the first time. Neither of us have had this experience before. And it allows you to just have so much. I wish I could understand it as a kid.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a357"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts387" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts389">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>And so I feel like it's allowed me to view you in a much more realistic way and just have so much more empathy for you and understanding and the ability to just see you for who you are outside of my mom. And I'm really grateful for that. [mmh] I feel like it's made me love you even more.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a358"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts391" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts393">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>and respect you even more.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a359"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts397" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts399">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>What does my love feel like?</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
